// Trusted-route guard: pause when the host reports its trusted network route
// unavailable. TRUSTED_ROUTE_DOWN is 0 while the route is verified reachable
// and 1 while it is not, published by the host's own connectivity check.
// REGIME: all of them. Route availability is a precondition, not a forecast,
// so this rule is armed whenever the system is running.
// CONDITIONS: none. The host is responsible for deciding what "the trusted
// route" is and for not publishing a 1 on a single dropped probe.
// INVALIDATION: the route coming back. This rule proposes a pause; it does not
// latch, and it does not decide when the pause ends - the host does.
// SHAPE: 1m; fast enough to notice a real outage, slow enough that a transient
// blip has already been smoothed away by the host's own measurement window.
// NOT endpoint_posture_ceiling: that counts how many endpoints are out of
// policy while the system still works. This one says the path itself is gone.
// CALIBRATED ON: nothing numeric to calibrate - the threshold is the host's
// own binary. What does not travel is the definition of "trusted", which is
// site-specific and belongs to whoever operates the network.
strategy TrustedRouteGuard {

    agent SecurityDesk

    every 1m {

        if TRUSTED_ROUTE_DOWN >= 1 {

            pause()

        }

    }

}
